---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Comments from '../../components/Comments.astro';
import WangcaiSeries from '../../components/WangcaiSeries.astro';
const P = 'https://media.huanyuchen.com/misc/coast-weekends/';
---
<BaseLayout title="Four Short Trips to the Coast — Huanyu Chen"
  description="Four short getaways near Los Angeles: a day in Santa Barbara and Solvang, a weekend in Avalon on Catalina Island, a June afternoon in Santa Barbara, and a Santa Barbara weekend with Wangcai that ended on the dunes at Mandalay Beach and a lunch in Malibu.">
  <p class="muted"><a href="/misc">← Misc</a> · March 2023 – June 2025</p>
  <h1>Four Short Trips to the Coast</h1>
  <p class="muted trip-sub">Danish pastries in Solvang, an island weekend, an afternoon in Santa Barbara, and a night by the dunes with Wangcai.</p>

  <div class="callout trip-stats">
    <strong>4 trips · March 2023, April 2024, June 2024, June 2025</strong><br />
    Santa Barbara and Solvang for a day (March 26, 2023) ·
    Avalon on Catalina Island, by ferry (April 20–21, 2024) ·
    Santa Barbara for an afternoon and evening (June 5, 2024) ·
    Santa Barbara, Zachari Dunes on Mandalay Beach in Oxnard, and lunch in Malibu (June 28–29, 2025)<br />
    One windmill · one heart-shaped Danish · one Casino that was never a casino · one semi-submersible over a kelp forest · one sand mermaid ·
    one cup of rolled ice cream · and Wangcai on the last one
  </div>

  <p>
    Not every trip from Los Angeles has to be a ten-hour drive. These four were short, close to home, and all by the
    sea: one day up the coast to Santa Barbara and Solvang, one weekend on an island, one afternoon up the coast, and one weekend from Santa Barbara down
    to the dunes at Oxnard. None of them had enough photos for a post of its own, so here they are together.
  </p>
  <p>
    As with the other posts here, everything below is reconstructed from the camera roll: what was photographed,
    where, and at what minute. For these four trips most of the photos are my wife's. I took very few.
  </p>

  <h2 id="sb-solvang-2023">Santa Barbara and Solvang · March 2023</h2>
  <p>
    The earliest of these was a day trip, on Sunday, March 26, 2023. We drove up the 101 along the coast and were in
    downtown <strong>Santa Barbara</strong> by 1:27, where a white Moorish-style building on State Street was trimmed
    with colored tile and a green bay window. After lunch we went down to the water by <strong>Stearns Wharf</strong>,
    and at 2:56 the city and the Santa Ynez Mountains stood across the water. Just before four we stopped at the lagoon
    at UCSB.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'sb2023-wharf-view.webp'} alt="Santa Barbara along the shore across blue water, with the Santa Ynez Mountains rising behind the town under a blue sky." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Santa Barbara and the mountains from the water, 2:56 pm.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'sb2023-state-st.webp'} alt="A white Moorish-style building with colored tile trim, a green bay window and a yellow door, with cactus and flowers in front." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>On State Street, 1:27 pm.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    Then we drove inland to <strong>Solvang</strong>, a town founded by Danish immigrants, with a windmill standing
    over a café patio.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig narrow-fig"><img src={P + 'solvang-windmill.webp'} alt="A white windmill with a dark cap and red lattice sails above a café patio with umbrellas, under a blue sky." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The windmill, 5:08 pm.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    By five we had a box of pastries, and at 5:21 a heart-shaped Danish with two jams. At 5:30 a pergola walkway was
    hung with wisteria in bloom.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'solvang-danish.webp'} alt="A heart-shaped Danish pastry with red and orange jam on a sheet of white paper, in front of a bakery case." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>A Danish, 5:21 pm.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'solvang-wisteria.webp'} alt="A white pergola over a shaded walkway, hung with purple wisteria, with a hedge and a sunlit path." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Wisteria over the walkway, 5:30 pm.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    We drove home down the 101, and at 7:09 the sun was setting in the side mirror.
  </p>

  <h2 id="avalon-2024">Avalon, Catalina Island · April 2024</h2>
  <figure class="trip-fig wide-fig">
    <a href={P + 'map-avalon.webp'}><img src={P + 'map-avalon.webp'} alt="Map of Avalon on Catalina Island: the ferry arriving by the Casino, a bluff east of town, Wrigley Terrace Road above the bay, the Wrigley Memorial up the canyon, Front Street, the Casino again on Sunday, and the semi-submersible tour by the pier." loading="lazy" /></a>
    <figcaption>Two days in Avalon. Each stop is placed where its photo was taken.</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <p>
    Avalon is the main town on Santa Catalina Island: a small, steep
    valley of houses and palms around a crescent-shaped harbor. We came over on the ferry on Saturday, April 20. At
    1:42 pm it was swinging into Avalon Bay, past a round white building on the point.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'avalon-casino-arrival.webp'} alt="A round white building with a red tile roof at the foot of a green headland, seen across blue water from an arriving boat, with small boats moored in front." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The Casino from the ferry, 1:42 pm.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'avalon-from-water.webp'} alt="Avalon spread along the shore below green hills, with boats moored in the bay and the ferry's wake in the foreground." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Avalon from the water, a minute later.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    That round white building is the <strong>Catalina Casino</strong>. Despite the name, it has never been a gambling
    hall: it holds a movie theater and a ballroom.
  </p>
  <p>
    We stayed at the <strong>Hotel St. Lauren</strong>, on the hill above town (it is also in
    <a href="/misc/hotels">the hotels post</a>). From there, at 2:09, the view ran down a street and over
    flat white roofs to the harbor, with a church bell tower just below.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'avalon-rooftops.webp'} alt="Looking down a street in Avalon lined with low white buildings and parked vehicles, past a white church bell tower with a cross, to the harbor and the sea." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Avalon's rooftops, 2:09 pm.</figcaption></figure>

  <h3>Up into the hills</h3>
  <p>
    Cars are scarce in Avalon, and most visitors get around in golf carts, which is how we spent the next hour or so.
    The first stop, at 3:16, was a bluff on the road east of town, looking out over open water at a two-masted
    schooner lying at anchor. A few minutes later we were climbing <strong>Wrigley Terrace Road</strong>, which winds up the hill on the east side of the bay.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'avalon-schooner.webp'} alt="A white two-masted schooner at anchor on calm blue water, seen from a rocky bluff." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>A schooner off the east end of town, 3:16 pm.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    The road climbs high above the water. At 3:29 I took the photo below: the coastline beyond the edge of the harbor, and green hills dropping straight into deep-blue water. Five minutes later, from farther along the road, the whole bay opened out: rows of white boats on their moorings, the Casino on its point, and a flag on
    the slope below us.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'avalon-coast-hills.webp'} alt="Green hillsides falling to a deep-blue sea, with the edge of Avalon harbor below and a headland in the distance." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Looking north from Wrigley Terrace Road, 3:29 pm.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'avalon-bay-casino.webp'} alt="Avalon Bay from above: rows of moored white boats, the round Casino on its point under a green headland, and an American flag on the slope below." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Avalon Bay and the Casino, 3:34 pm.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    Near the top, on the seaward side, a big eucalyptus leans out over the slope with a rope swing hanging from one
    branch, and a plain white cross stands at the edge.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'avalon-swing.webp'} alt="A rope swing hanging from a pale eucalyptus branch over a wide blue ocean." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The swing, 3:41 pm.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'avalon-cross.webp'} alt="A white wooden cross beside eucalyptus branches, with the sea and a few boats below." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The white cross, 3:45 pm.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    Far below, a yellow semi-submersible was carrying a load of visitors along the shore. We took a
    semi-submersible tour ourselves the next day.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig narrow-fig"><img src={P + 'avalon-yellow-sub-above.webp'} alt="A small yellow semi-submersible boat full of passengers on deep-blue water, seen from a cliff top." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>A yellow semi-sub below, 3:40 pm.</figcaption></figure>

  <h3>The Wrigley Memorial</h3>
  <p>
    From the hills we drove inland, up Avalon Canyon, to the <strong>Wrigley Memorial</strong>, at 4:14. It is a tall
    monument to William Wrigley Jr. at the head of the canyon, decorated with Catalina tile. Through the
    tall arch at the top you see only sky and a sliver of sea. Turn around and the tiled parapet frames the whole
    canyon, running down to the water.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'wrigley-arch.webp'} alt="A tall stone arch with a blue mosaic ceiling, framing blue sky and a sliver of sea." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The arch at the top of the memorial, 4:14 pm.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'wrigley-tiles.webp'} alt="A parapet of blue and patterned tiles in front of a green canyon that runs down to a strip of sea." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Catalina tile, and the canyon down to the sea.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>

  <h3>Evening on the waterfront</h3>
  <p>
    By 4:49 we were back down on <strong>Front Street</strong>, the waterfront promenade, which on a sunny Saturday
    was full of people walking under the palms between the shops and the beach. Around five the light began to
    soften, and a long bank of cloud settled over the hills behind the harbor.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'avalon-front-street.webp'} alt="A busy waterfront promenade lined with tall palms and shopfronts, with people walking and the harbor at the end." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Front Street, 4:49 pm.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'avalon-evening.webp'} alt="Houses on a hillside above a pier and a small harbor, with a bank of white cloud lying over the green hills behind." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Cloud over the hills, 5:02 pm.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    Dinner, around six, was on the waterfront: seared scallops on an orange sauce, among other things. When we came
    out, at 7:13, the moon was already up over the palms, a few days short of full, above the strings of bulbs and
    the houses on the hill.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'avalon-dinner.webp'} alt="Three seared scallops on an orange sauce with drizzles of cream and chopped chives, on a rectangular plate." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Scallops at dinner, 6:04 pm.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'avalon-moon.webp'} alt="A small white moon in a pale evening sky above tall palms and strings of lights, with houses on a hillside below." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The moon over Avalon, 7:13 pm.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>

  <h3>Sunday: under the water</h3>
  <p>
    Sunday began grayer. The balcony view that morning, with a cruise ship anchored offshore, is in
    <a href="/misc/hotels">the hotels post</a>. At 11:22 we walked out to the Casino: a ring of arches around the top, flags all along the roof, and the sea right up against its base.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'casino-morning.webp'} alt="The round white Catalina Casino close up, with a ring of arches near the top and flags along the roof, standing over the water under a gray sky." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The Casino, 11:22 am.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    At midday we took a <strong>semi-submersible tour</strong> from the pier. A semi-submersible is a
    boat whose cabin sits below the waterline, with windows down the sides, so you sit under the surface while the
    deck stays above it. Just off Avalon is a forest of giant kelp, and from 12:58 the windows were full of it: tall
    golden-brown stalks rising out of the blue, lit from above.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'kelp-forest.webp'} alt="Underwater view through a window of tall golden-brown kelp stalks rising through blue water." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The kelp forest, 1:09 pm.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    Then came the fish. At 1:10 a whole school was crowding the windows: big dark fish in front, and
    behind them dozens of smaller, speckled ones, kelp bass and others I can't name. The last photo of them, at 1:24,
    is the clearest: one large dark fish broadside to the
    window, with spotted bass all around it.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'kelp-fish.webp'} alt="A crowd of fish seen through an underwater window: large dark fish in front and many smaller silvery and speckled fish behind." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The school arrives, 1:10 pm.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'kelp-bass.webp'} alt="A large dark fish swimming broadside past an underwater window, surrounded by smaller spotted fish in green-blue water." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Up close, 1:24 pm.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    Back in the bay, at 1:36, the cruise ship from the morning was still at anchor: Holland America's <em>Zaandam</em>. We took the ferry back to the
    mainland around six. By 7:22 the fog had closed in, and the ferry was passing container ships off Long Beach.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'avalon-cruise-ship.webp'} alt="A large white and dark-blue cruise ship named Zaandam anchored on gray water under an overcast sky." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The <em>Zaandam</em> at anchor, 1:36 pm.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'ferry-container-ship.webp'} alt="A container ship stacked with containers, marked MSC on its hull, on gray water in fog." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Fog and a container ship on the way back, 7:22 pm.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>

  <h2 id="sb-2024">An afternoon in Santa Barbara · June 2024</h2>
  <p>
    We had been to Santa Barbara together once before, in March 2023, on the way to Solvang; my first visit was at the
    end of the first of the <a href="/misc/roadtrips-2022">2022 road trips</a>, an evening on Stearns Wharf. This visit, on Wednesday,
    June 5, was short, from mid-afternoon to evening, and only a handful of photos survive. The first, at 2:44, is of
    an angel's trumpet in full bloom beside a building in town, hung all over with long yellow flowers like bells.
    At 3:07 we stopped for a pair of steamed bao.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'sb-angels-trumpet.webp'} alt="A shrub covered in long hanging yellow trumpet-shaped flowers, beside a building, with mountains in the distance." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Angel's trumpet in bloom, 2:44 pm.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'sb-bao.webp'} alt="Two steamed bao filled with glazed meat, cucumber, carrot and cilantro on a white plate, with jars of pickled peppers and chili behind." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Bao, 3:07 pm.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    In the evening we went down to the beach by Stearns Wharf. At 8:23, someone had left a mermaid in the sand: a
    sculpture of a woman lying on her front, her long hair spread out and a tail curling away behind her. Dinner, after
    nine, was in the <strong>Funk Zone</strong>, the neighborhood of tasting rooms and restaurants between the
    freeway and the beach.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig narrow-fig"><img src={P + 'sb-sand-mermaid.webp'} alt="A sand sculpture of a mermaid lying on the beach, with long hair and a curling tail." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>A sand mermaid, 8:23 pm.</figcaption></figure>

  <h2 id="sb-2025">Santa Barbara and the dunes, with Wangcai · June 2025</h2>
  <figure class="trip-fig wide-fig">
    <a href={P + 'map-coast-2025.webp'}><img src={P + 'map-coast-2025.webp'} alt="Map of the coast from Santa Barbara to Malibu: lunch, Rolld ice cream at Paseo Nuevo and dinner in Santa Barbara, Zachari Dunes on Mandalay Beach in Oxnard, and Geoffrey's Malibu." loading="lazy" /></a>
    <figcaption>One weekend, from Santa Barbara down the coast to Malibu.</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <p>
    A year later we went back, this time with <a href="/misc/wangcai-2025">Wangcai</a>, who had come home that
    January. Lunch on Saturday, June 28, came with a tiki drink in a big glass, topped with an orchid and a charred half-shell of lime.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig narrow-fig"><img src={P + 'sb-tiki-drink.webp'} alt="A frosted tiki drink in a large round glass, topped with a pink orchid and a charred lime shell, on a weathered wooden table." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Lunch, 12:58 pm.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    In the afternoon we stopped at <strong>Rolld Ice Cream</strong> in Paseo Nuevo, the open-air shopping street off
    State Street. Rolled ice cream is made on a frozen steel plate: the cream is poured out, mixed with the toppings,
    spread thin and scraped up into tight rolls, which are then stood on end in the cup. The board inside listed that
    week's rotating flavors, for June 28 to July 4, with Dubai chocolate pistachio among them. We sat outside on the
    brick patio, where Wangcai posed beside the cup with her tongue out.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig narrow-fig"><img src={P + 'sb-wangcai-rolld.webp'} alt="A cream-colored dog with her tongue out sitting on a brick patio beside a café table with a cup of pink rolled ice cream topped with whipped cream." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Wangcai and the rolled ice cream, 3:45 pm.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    Dinner, at 8:06, was a whole grilled fish with broccolini and a little pot of blistered cherry tomatoes. Then we
    drove down the coast in the dark to Oxnard, where we were staying at <strong>Zachari Dunes on Mandalay
    Beach</strong>, a Spanish-style resort that sits right on the dunes (it is in <a href="/misc/hotels">the hotels
    post</a> too).
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'sb-grilled-fish.webp'} alt="A whole charred grilled fish on a blue plate with broccolini, sliced peppers and a small pot of cherry tomatoes." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Grilled fish at dinner, 8:06 pm.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    At 8:26 on Sunday morning, Wangcai was out on the balcony under
    the pergola, looking over the wall at the dunes, the palms and the sea. A quarter of an hour later the pool was
    still empty except for one seagull on the edge.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'zachari-wangcai-balcony.webp'} alt="A cream-colored dog standing on a hotel balcony under a wooden pergola, looking out at dunes, palm trees and the ocean." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Wangcai on the balcony, 8:26 am.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'zachari-pool.webp'} alt="A turquoise pool in front of a row of empty loungers and a folded umbrella, with a seagull standing among the chairs and a rock waterfall behind." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The pool and its only guest, 8:40 am.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    By 10:58 the sky was starting to clear, and the view from the window was of a wide, nearly empty beach, with palms
    planted along the top of the sand.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'zachari-beach-window.webp'} alt="A window framing a wide sandy beach with tall palms, low dunes and the ocean under soft clouds." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Mandalay Beach from the window, 10:58 am.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    On the way home we stopped for lunch at <strong>Geoffrey's Malibu</strong>, which is built on a cliff above the
    Pacific Coast Highway. The terrace looks over the roofs of the beach houses below and a bank of bougainvillea to
    the ocean, and lunch was the house paella.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'geoffreys-terrace.webp'} alt="View from a restaurant terrace between two canvas umbrellas: beach-house roofs, pink bougainvillea and potted flowers above a calm blue ocean." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The terrace at Geoffrey's Malibu, 1:29 pm.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    By evening we were back home. Four trips, none longer than one night away: a day of pastries in Solvang, an island, an afternoon on the beach, and a weekend with the dog, all within easy reach of Los Angeles.
  </p>

  <p class="muted"><small>Maps built from the trips' photo timestamps and locations. Photo locations have been removed
